Competitive Pressure and the Completion of Pre-Deployment Safety Evaluation: A Partial-Identification Theory under Projective Observation

Ouadi Maakoul and SI TEAM

---

Abstract

This manuscript is a purely theoretical treatment of whether the public record can support inference about the effect of competitive pressure on the completion of pre-deployment safety evaluation in frontier AI. No empirical measurement, laboratory experiment, or computational training is undertaken. The object of study is the inferential architecture itself: what a selected, projectively observed record can and cannot identify, and what an auditable reporting instrument would have to look like given that structure. The central formal object is an observation map that records the product Z = MY of completion Y and disclosure M together with a provenance-annotated document lattice. Three impossibility results organise the theory. First, the completion probability is not identified on any class of data-generating processes that permits undisclosed completion, and a finite-sample minimax lower bound is proved for any estimator under the Λ-unbounded class. Second, because deployment timing is responsive to the same pressure that the hypothesis predicts affects completion, deployment censoring is informative rather than independent, and the observed conditional completion probability can reverse sign relative to the structural effect within a race-dominant class of release-timing models; a two-type construction establishes the reversal and delimits the class for which the structural sign is well-defined. Third, sharp partial-identification bounds are derived for the completion difference under a bounded-odds-of-nondisclosure model, together with a breakdown quantity measuring the undisclosed mass required to reverse the sign; the bounds are sharp under separability of the group models and remain valid as outer bounds under dependence, with the sharper joint-bound characterisation given. The admissibility rule is formalised as a closure operator on a lattice of provenance-graded documents, with the tier convention built into the definition rather than into the proof, yielding extensivity, monotonicity, and idempotence together with audit-stability corollaries. Inference relies on a stratified studentised permutation test whose exactness is proved for the sharp null on the observed outcomes rather than on the latent completion indicators, with a closed-form minimum detectable effect under the Fisher approximation and an explicit finite-sample refinement. Impossibility theorems establish that the Blackwell order admits no scalar representation via an explicit incomparable pair, that the structural release rule is not identified from timing marginals, and that the optimal review-window functional is unidentified without welfare parameters. A layered architecture with a determinism theorem and a record-monotonicity theorem guarantees that the report is a reproducible function of the frozen record and frozen parameters, and that adding documentation cannot silently overturn a passing audit. A constructive agenda states the minimal additional observables that would restore point identification, converting the impossibility results into a specification for the disclosure regime rather than a verdict on it. The design's primary expected outcome is an audit failure, which is reported as a measurement of the disclosure regime rather than as a procedural defect.

Keywords: partial identification, informative censoring, non-ignorable missingness, randomization inference, closure operators, identifiability, auditability

---

1. Introduction

A recurring claim in AI governance asserts that competition between frontier developers compresses or weakens pre-deployment safety evaluation. The claim is theoretically underdetermined: formal models of release timing admit both a race to the bottom, in which first-mover advantage induces premature deployment, and insufficient entry, in which developers delay to free-ride on rivals' safety experimentation. The sign of the comparative static is a condition of the model rather than a theorem of it. This manuscript does not resolve the indeterminacy by assertion. It characterises, mathematically, the conditions under which any resolution is identifiable from the public record, and it proves that under natural assumptions those conditions fail.

The manuscript is a theory of inference, not an empirical study. Its primitives are a record, an observation map, a target functional, and a class of data-generating processes. Its results are non-identifiability theorems, sharp and outer partial-identification bounds, exactness and power results for a permutation procedure, impossibility theorems for excluded estimands, and correctness and complexity statements for algorithms that compute the identified sets. Empirical material is replaced by explicit assumptions, stated in Section 3 and labelled as premises rather than findings. Every theorem is stated with an explicit "under assumptions …" clause so that its dependence on the premises is immediate. Algorithms are given as mathematical specifications with correctness and complexity statements; no code is executed and no data are processed.

Two methodological choices distinguish this treatment from the measurement-design literature it responds to. First, the exposure is defined at evaluation start rather than at release, because a release-date definition conflates the pressure experienced during the evaluation with the length of the evaluation itself. Second, the deployment event is separated from the disclosure event: deployment is an observable censoring mechanism handled by conditioning, whereas disclosure is a non-observable missingness mechanism handled by partial identification. Conflating the two is the principal inferential error the theory is designed to prevent, and Lemma 1 makes the separation formal.

The central architectural commitment is that the record is a projection. It reveals a coarsening of the latent variables, not the latent variables themselves. The theory's contribution is to state precisely what can be recovered from the projection, what cannot, and what audit functionals are well-defined and reproducible regardless. The impossibility results are not obstacles to be overcome by better statistics; they are properties of the observation map. The partial-identification results are the appropriate positive objects once point identification fails. The audit is the appropriate primary object once the policy-relevant question is recast as a question about the disclosure regime rather than a question about a single structural parameter.

