5. Partial Identification

Definition 2 (Identified set). For a class \mathcal{P}(\Lambda) of data-generating processes satisfying A1, A2 and A5, and for a functional \theta: \mathcal{P} \to \mathbb{R}, the identified set is

\Theta(\Lambda) = \big\{ \theta(P) : P \in \mathcal{P}(\Lambda),\ P \text{ induces the observed law on } (Z, G) \big\}.

For the group-specific completion probability, write

p_g = P(Z = 1 \mid G \in \mathcal{G}_g), \qquad \pi_g = P(Y = 1 \mid G \in \mathcal{G}_g).

Theorem 4 (Sharp group bounds). Under A1, A2 and A5, the set

\Pi_g(\Lambda) = \big[\, p_g,\ \min\{1,\ p_g(1+\Lambda)\} \,\big]

is the sharp identified set for \pi_g.

Proof. By A5, the undisclosed mass is a_g = \pi_g - p_g \ge 0 with a_g / p_g = \lambda_g \le \Lambda, so \pi_g \le p_g(1+\Lambda); also \pi_g \le 1 since it is a probability, and \pi_g \ge p_g since a_g \ge 0. For sharpness, fix any \pi \in \Pi_g(\Lambda) and set

P(Y = 1, M = 1 \mid \mathcal{G}_g) = p_g, \quad
P(Y = 1, M = 0 \mid \mathcal{G}_g) = \pi - p_g, \quad
P(Y = 0 \mid \mathcal{G}_g) = 1 - \pi.

All three quantities are non-negative by the constraints on \pi, and they sum to one. The induced P(Z = 1 \mid \mathcal{G}_g) = p_g, and the implied odds ratio is (\pi - p_g)/p_g \le \Lambda. Hence \pi is attained. \square

Corollary 3 (Minkowski-difference bounds on the difference). Under the assumptions of Theorem 4, the set

\Delta \in \Big[\, p_{\text{lo}} - \min\{1, p_{\text{hi}}(1+\Lambda)\},\ \min\{1, p_{\text{lo}}(1+\Lambda)\} - p_{\text{hi}} \,\Big]

contains the identified set for \Delta on any class satisfying A1, A2 and A5. The containment is sharp under A8 (independence of groups); under a shared dependence structure that constrains the joint law of (Z_{\text{lo}}, Z_{\text{hi}}) beyond the marginals, the sharper bound is obtained by solving the linear programme in Proposition 1.

Proof. The bounds are the Minkowski difference of the two group identified sets and therefore contain the identified set of the difference for any dependence structure consistent with the marginals. Sharpness under independence follows because the separability of the group constraints means every pair (\pi_{\text{lo}}, \pi_{\text{hi}}) \in \Pi_{\text{lo}}(\Lambda) \times \Pi_{\text{hi}}(\Lambda) is attainable, and the Minkowski difference is the image of the product set under the map (\pi_{\text{lo}}, \pi_{\text{hi}}) \mapsto \pi_{\text{lo}} - \pi_{\text{hi}}. \square

Proposition 1 (Joint-law sharp bound). Suppose the observed joint law of (Z_{\text{lo}}, Z_{\text{hi}}) is fixed, and let \kappa = P(Z_{\text{lo}} = 1, Z_{\text{hi}} = 1) be the observed co-occurrence (which for disjoint release groups is a function of the marginal assignment, not of pairing). The sharp identified set for \Delta is the value set of the linear programme

\min / \max\ \Delta = \pi_{\text{lo}} - \pi_{\text{hi}} \quad \text{s.t.}\quad
\sum_{(M_{\text{lo}}, Y_{\text{lo}})} \!\!\!\! P(M_{\text{lo}}, Y_{\text{lo}}, M_{\text{hi}}, Y_{\text{hi}}) = \text{target marginals,}

subject to the odds constraints P(M_g = 0, Y_g = 1) \le \Lambda\, P(M_g = 1, Y_g = 1) and the observation constraints P(M_g = 1, Y_g = 1) = p_g for each g, together with the joint feasibility constraints that any shared dependence structure imposes on the four-way table.

Proof. The identification region is the image of a polytope defined by linear equalities and inequalities, and linear functionals attain extrema at vertices of polytopes; the sharp bounds are those vertex values. \square

When A8 is maintained, the joint feasibility constraint reduces to factorization and the polytope's value set coincides with the Minkowski difference of Corollary 3. The proposition is included so that the sharp bound under dependence is stated and not merely gestured at, and because the manuscript's coding unit is the release, not the developer, so a within-developer correlation structure is a plausible departure from A8 that should be made formally tractable.

Definition 3 (Breakdown bound). The breakdown bound is

\Lambda^{*} = \inf\big\{ \Lambda \ge 0 : \underline{\Delta}(\Lambda) \le 0 \le \overline{\Delta}(\Lambda) \big\},

where \underline{\Delta}, \overline{\Delta} are the endpoints of Corollary 3.

\Lambda^{*} is the quantity reported in place of a significance test. A small \Lambda^{*} means that a modest amount of completed-but-undisclosed evaluation would suffice to reverse the sign; a large \Lambda^{*} means that the sign is robust to substantial undisclosed mass. When \Lambda^{*} = \infty the sign is determined even at the trivial bound, which can occur only if the observed probabilities themselves are extreme.

Proposition 2 (Monotone-selection refinement). Suppose in addition that the disclosure odds are ordered across groups, \lambda_{\text{hi}} \le \lambda_{\text{lo}}, where \lambda_{\text{hi}} corresponds to the high-pressure group. Then the identified set is contained in the set obtained by imposing \lambda_{\text{hi}} = \lambda_{\text{lo}} = \lambda \le \Lambda, and the difference bounds tighten to

\Delta \in \Big[\, p_{\text{lo}}(1+\lambda^{*}) - p_{\text{hi}}(1+\Lambda),\ p_{\text{lo}}(1+\Lambda) - p_{\text{hi}}(1+\lambda^{*}) \,\Big]

with \lambda^{*} = \max\{0, p_{\text{hi}}^{-1} - 1\} the smallest feasible common odds consistent with the observed p_{\text{hi}}.

Proof. The equality case follows by solving the two equations p_g(1+\lambda) = \pi_g for a common \lambda subject to \pi_g \le 1. The inequality case follows since \lambda_{\text{hi}} \le \lambda_{\text{lo}} \le \Lambda restricts the pair (\lambda_{\text{hi}}, \lambda_{\text{lo}}) to a simplex whose extreme points generate the stated interval. \square

Proposition 2 is the formal counterpart of Lee-type trimming. It is stated as a proposition rather than a theorem because the ordering assumption is substantive and not testable from the record.

Remark 2 (Percentile imputation is not a bound). Imputing the missing outcomes at empirical percentiles of the observed distribution produces neither a sharp bound nor a valid confidence set; it is a sensitivity exercise whose output depends on the percentile chosen. The support bounds of Theorem 4 are sharp and assumption-explicit; the percentile exercise may be reported only under the label sensitivity analysis.

