7. Inference: Stratified Randomization

Let strata k = 1, \ldots, K correspond to developers, with n_k releases and n_{1k} in the high-pressure group, \sum_k n_k = n. The assignment W = (W_i) ranges over the orbit

\Omega(W) = \Big\{ w \in \{0,1\}^{n} : \textstyle\sum_{i \in \text{stratum } k} w_i = n_{1k} \ \forall k \Big\}.

Definition 7 (Sharp null and statistic). The sharp null on the observed outcomes is H_0^{Z}: Z_i(1) = Z_i(0) for all i. The statistic is

T(W, Z) = \frac{\hat\Delta(W, Z)}{\sqrt{\hat\sigma_1^2(W,Z)/n_1 + \hat\sigma_0^2(W,Z)/n_0}},

with \hat\Delta the stratified difference in means and \hat\sigma_g^2 the within-group sample variances.

Theorem 6 (Exactness on the observed outcomes). Under H_0^{Z}, for any \alpha \in (0,1),

P_{H_0^{Z}}\big(p_{\text{perm}} \le \alpha\big) \le \alpha,

where p_{\text{perm}} = |\Omega(W)|^{-1} \sum_{w \in \Omega(W)} \mathbf{1}\{|T(w,Z)| \ge |T(W,Z)|\}, and the probability is over the randomization of W and, when the test is randomized, over the auxiliary randomization.

Proof. Under H_0^{Z}, Z is fixed and W is uniform on \Omega(W) conditional on the within-stratum treated counts. For each realized assignment \omega \in \Omega(W), the rank of |T(\omega, Z)| among \{|T(w, Z)| : w \in \Omega(W)\} is uniform on \{1, \ldots, |\Omega(W)|\}, because the ranks are a permutation of the integers. Hence P_{H_0^{Z}}(p_{\text{perm}} \le \alpha) \le \lfloor \alpha |\Omega(W)| \rfloor / |\Omega(W)| \le \alpha. \square

Corollary 6 (No power against the structural hypothesis). The permutation test of Definition 7 has power equal to its size against any departure from H_0^{Z} that arises from the latent completion mechanism alone, that is, against any alternative in which Y_i differs from Z_i but Z_i is unchanged. Consequently, no permutation p-value against H_0^{Z} can be evidence for or against the structural hypothesis that Theorem 3 shows is not identified.

Proof. Since T is a function of Z alone, any two data-generating processes that induce the same observed law on Z produce the same test statistic and the same p-value. The alternatives in question induce the same Z-law, so power equals size. \square

Corollary 6 is the reason the design reports the permutation p-value as a description of the observed outcomes, never as evidence for the structural hypothesis.

Proposition 3 (Necessity of studentization). Under H_0^{Z} with unequal variances \sigma_1^2 \neq \sigma_0^2 and unequal group sizes, the unstudentized statistic T_0(W, Z) = \hat\Delta(W, Z) has asymptotic size strictly greater than \alpha for \alpha < 1/2 along sequences with n_k \to \infty and n_{1k}/n_k \to c_k \in (0,1).

Proof sketch. The permutation distribution of \hat\Delta has variance

\operatorname{Var}_{\text{perm}}(\hat\Delta) = \frac{\sigma^2}{n}\left(\frac{1}{c} + \frac{1}{1-c}\right)

with \sigma^2 the pooled variance, whereas the sampling variance is \sigma_1^2/(nc) + \sigma_0^2/(n(1-c)). The two coincide only if \sigma_1^2 = \sigma_0^2. When they differ, the permutation distribution is too narrow or too wide relative to the sampling distribution, and the test over- or under-rejects. Studentization replaces the pooled variance with a group-specific estimate and restores asymptotic pivoting. \square

Proposition 4 (Bootstrap failure with few treated clusters). Let K_1 be the number of treated clusters. The cluster-robust variance estimator is downward-biased by a factor of order K_1/(K_1 - 1), and the wild cluster bootstrap with Rademacher weights has size bounded away from \alpha uniformly over data-generating processes in the neighbourhood of the null when 2^{K_1} is small enough that the bootstrap distribution cannot approximate the sampling distribution in the tails at level \alpha.

Proof sketch. The bias of the cluster-robust estimator follows from the expectation of the squared cluster residual, which undershoots the true cluster variance by the leverage of the cluster mean. The bootstrap failure follows because the Rademacher distribution with K_1 clusters has support of size 2^{K_1}; when 2^{K_1} is small enough that the (1-\alpha) quantile of the bootstrap distribution cannot resolve the tail mass at \alpha, the bootstrap test cannot control size. The specific numerical threshold is a conventional heuristic, not a theoretical constant. \square

Propositions 3 and 4 together justify the design choice: randomization inference is primary, studentization is retained, and the wild cluster bootstrap is reported as a descriptive complement whose disagreement with the permutation test is a caveat rather than a resolution.

