9. Impossibility Results for Excluded Estimands

Theorem 8 (No scalar representation of the Blackwell order). Let experiments be channels P_{Y \mid X} with |X| = |Y| = 2. The Blackwell order is partial, and there exist pairs of experiments that are incomparable. Consequently no continuous scalar functional I(P_{Y\mid X}) represents the order.

Proof. Let

A = \begin{pmatrix} 1 & 0 \\ 0.5 & 0.5 \end{pmatrix}, \qquad
B = \begin{pmatrix} 0.5 & 0.5 \\ 0 & 1 \end{pmatrix}.

The Blackwell–Marshak lemma states that A \succeq B iff B = A G for a stochastic G whose rows are probability vectors. The identity B = A G requires, for each column j,

G_{0j} = B_{0j}, \qquad \tfrac{1}{2} G_{0j} + \tfrac{1}{2} G_{1j} = B_{1j}.

For j = 0: G_{00} = 0.5 and 0.25 + 0.5 G_{10} = 0, so G_{10} = -0.5 < 0, not stochastic. For j = 1: G_{01} = 0.5 and 0.25 + 0.5 G_{11} = 1, so G_{11} = 1.5 > 1, not stochastic. Hence A \not\succeq B. The identity A = B G requires

\tfrac{1}{2} G_{0j} + \tfrac{1}{2} G_{1j} = A_{0j}, \qquad G_{1j} = A_{1j}.

For j = 0: G_{10} = 0.5 and 0.5 G_{00} + 0.25 = 1, so G_{00} = 1.5 > 1, not stochastic. For j = 1: G_{11} = 0.5 and 0.5 G_{01} + 0.25 = 0, so G_{01} = -0.5 < 0, not stochastic. Hence B \not\succeq A. The pair is incomparable. A continuous scalar representation would induce a total preorder extending the partial order and would therefore rank the pair; since no ranking exists, no such representation exists. \square

Theorem 8 is a stronger statement than "the order is partial": it certifies that the partiality is not an artefact of coarse examples but is generated by pairs of 2 \times 2 experiments that any realistic coding would encounter.

Theorem 9 (Non-identifiability of the structural release rule). Let \mathcal{M} be the class of release-timing models satisfying A4, each with a best-response function \sigma_m(\pi, u) and a type-distribution F_m. The marginal distribution of observed release times does not identify \sigma_m.

Proof. For two models m_1, m_2 with best-response functions \sigma_{m_1} \neq \sigma_{m_2}, the marginal is

P_m(t) = \int P\big(t \mid u; \sigma_{m}\big) \, dF_m(u).

Choose F_{m_1} \neq F_{m_2} to satisfy P_{m_1}(t) = P_{m_2}(t) for all t; such a pair exists whenever the map (F, \sigma) \mapsto P is not injective, which holds whenever the family \{P(\cdot \mid u; \sigma_m)\}_u admits a mixture representation with two distinct mixing measures, and this holds for the race and free-riding families because they are convex combinations over the same latent types. Since the record supplies only the marginal, the pair (F_m, \sigma_m) is not recoverable. \square

Theorem 9 justifies the exclusion of structural inversion: the comparative static \partial \tau^{*} / \partial G is not a functional of the record.

Theorem 10 (Non-identifiability of the optimal window). Let \mathcal{W} be the feasible set of review-window lengths and let \Omega be the class of admissible welfare-and-delay cost parameters. If \Omega is unrestricted, the identified set of optimal windows is \mathcal{W} itself.

Proof. For any w_0 \in \mathcal{W}, choose U(\cdot; \omega) \in \{U(\cdot; \omega) : \omega \in \Omega\} with a strict maximum at w_0; such a functional exists for any w_0 when \Omega is unrestricted, since the class contains indicator-like functionals with a peak at any point. Then w_0 is optimal for \omega, so w_0 belongs to the identified set. Since w_0 was arbitrary, the identified set is \mathcal{W}. \square

Theorem 10 justifies reporting the observed windows only as a descriptive comparison against the proposed anchors, never as an optimality claim.

