12. Discussion

12.1 What the theory establishes and what it does not

The theory delivers a specific verdict on the inferential question. The completion probability is not identified from the public record under any class of data-generating processes that permits undisclosed completion (Theorem 1), a finite-sample minimax lower bound holds for any estimator on the Λ-unbounded class (Theorem 2), and the observed conditional probability can reverse sign relative to the structural effect within the race-dominant class (Theorem 3). These are properties of the observation map, not obstacles that better statistics could remove. The only defensible inferential objects are the identified sets of Section 5, the breakdown bound \Lambda^{*}, and the audit quantities of Section 6.

Three consequences follow. First, the design's primary estimand is the audit functional, not the completion difference. The audit is a deterministic, monotone, reproducible function of the record (Theorems 11 and 12), and its failure is a measurement of the disclosure regime rather than a defect of the study. Second, the descriptive completion difference is reported only as a description, with bounds and a breakdown quantity, and never as evidence for or against the structural hypothesis (Corollary 6). Third, the survival model is retained only as an exploratory summary, because its independent-censoring assumption is contradicted by the hypothesis itself (Lemma 1 and Theorem 3).

12.2 The role of Λ

Assumption A5 bounds the undisclosed odds, and the breakdown bound \Lambda^{*} reports how much undisclosed mass would suffice to reverse the sign. The programme does not pretend to estimate \Lambda from the record, because Theorem 2 forbids it. It reports \Lambda^{*} and leaves the judgment of whether \Lambda^{*} is small or large to the reader, with the explicit statement that this judgment is substantive and not statistical.

Reference values for \Lambda may be supplied as sensitivity benchmarks, but they are not estimates. Two natural candidates are the silent-revision rates reported in the safety-framework literature—approximately two-thirds of material changes silent under a strict standard, with tightening and loosening roughly balanced—and a simple upper bound derived from the maximum plausible fraction of completed-but-undisclosed evaluations consistent with the observed deployment behaviour. Both are calibrated to adjacent rather than to the same object: revision silence is not the same quantity as evaluation-duration missingness, and the mapping between them is an assumption. The reference values should therefore be reported alongside \Lambda^{*} with the assumption stated, not as calibrations of \Lambda.

12.3 The theoretical indeterminacy is not resolved by the design

Theorems 1–3 do not resolve the Choi–Jeon–Menicucci indeterminacy between race and free-riding equilibria. They characterise what the record can say conditional on which class the data-generating process lies in, and they show that the observed conditional is consistent with both classes. The behavioural literature on AI races, which finds that falling behind increases unsafe play in a laboratory setting, motivates the race-dominant class A3' as a plausible maintained hypothesis. It does not identify it. The design is honest about this: the structural sign is stated as a hypothesis, the observed sign is treated as descriptive, and the gap between them is a partial-identification interval whose width is measured by \Lambda^{*}.

12.4 The impossibility results delimit the programme

The Blackwell order admits no scalar representation (Theorem 8), so no composite informativeness index is estimable from documents that do not record evaluator posteriors. The structural release rule is not identified from timing marginals (Theorem 9), so structural inversion is unavailable. The optimal review window is unidentified without welfare parameters (Theorem 10), so the policy anchors are used only descriptively. These exclusions are theorems, not conveniences, and they should be treated as boundaries of the research programme rather than as gaps to be filled by future work in the same direction.

12.5 The audit functional as primary object

The primary scientific contribution is the triple (N_{\text{adm}}, N_{\text{labs}}, p_{\text{indep}}) together with the disclosure-gap report—the histogram of missing fields, the fraction of releases lacking an admitted start–deployment pair, and the list of regime changes that would close the gap. These quantities are deterministic, monotone in the record, reproducible under the freeze operator, and independent of the hypothesis-testing apparatus. They answer a well-posed question: can the public record support inference about the competition–safety relationship at the stated standard? The answer is likely no, and reporting that answer with the same prominence a hypothesis test would receive is the design's substantive contribution. The partial-identification bounds on \Delta are secondary descriptive objects whose interpretation is deliberately limited by Theorems 1–3.

