13. Constructive Agenda: Minimal Additional Observables

The impossibility results of Sections 4 and 9 are not verdicts on the hypothesis; they are statements about what the record would need to contain for the hypothesis to become estimable. Each of the following additions to the observation map would restore point identification, tighten the identified set, or convert an outer bound into a sharp one. They are stated as minimal extensions in the sense that removing any one of them leaves at least one theorem's conclusion unchanged.

(C1) Mandatory disclosure of completion status. If a completion statement is required for every evaluation that was initiated, whether or not it was published, then M_i \equiv 1 on the admitted set and the projection Z = MY collapses to Y. Theorems 1 and 2 become vacuous, \Pi_g(\Lambda) collapses to a point, and the permutation test of Section 7 acquires power against the structural hypothesis through the observed Y. The requirement is strictly on the existence of the completion statement, not on its content, so it does not force disclosure of evaluator conclusions.

(C2) Contractual independence of publication rights from pressure. If the independence regime \iota_i is fixed at the outset of the evaluation and is not adjustable in response to competitive conditions, then the regime stratifier is exogenous to the exposure and the partial-identification bounds sharpen. The weaker requirement, sufficient for the design as stated, is that any change to \iota_i be dated and disclosed. The stronger requirement—that \iota_i be fixed at evaluation start—would make the regime a pre-determined covariate and permit a version of Theorem 3 in which the type-composition mechanism operates only through capacity, not through legal review.

(C3) Standardised reporting of evaluation start dates. If s_i is reported for every evaluation, the conditioning event \mathcal{A}^{S} expands to \mathcal{A} and the theorems of Section 4 apply to the full admissible set rather than to the subset with documented start dates. The current theory restricts itself to \mathcal{A}^{S} because G_i is otherwise unobservable; standardised start-date reporting removes the restriction.

(C4) Bounded \Lambda via transparency requirements. If disclosure is required to be audited—for example, by an independent body that certifies the fraction of completed evaluations that were published—then the parameter \Lambda is bounded above by the inverse of the certified disclosure fraction. The identified set of Theorem 4 sharpens and the breakdown bound \Lambda^{*} becomes interpretable as a policy-relevant quantity: the level of undisclosed mass above which the sign is not determined by the data.

(C5) Enumeration duty on safety-framework revisions. If every material revision of a safety framework must enumerate what changed—not why—then the silent-revision rate is directly observable and supplies a reference value for the adjacent-object calibration of \Lambda. The requirement is strictly weaker than a justification duty and is compatible with trade-secret protection of the underlying rationale.

These five extensions are not mutually exclusive and their combination would restore point identification of the completion probability under A1. The programme's contribution is to state, in theorem form, precisely which extension is doing which work: (C1) removes Theorems 1 and 2, (C3) expands the domain of every G-theorem, (C4) sharpens the partial-identification bounds, and (C2) and (C5) tighten the dependence of the bounds on the disclosure regime. A governance mechanism that adopts (C1) and (C3) alone would make the question of Section 1 estimable in the strong sense; the other three improve the precision and the Competitive Pressure and the Completion of Pre-Deployment Safety Evaluation: A Partial-Identification Theory under Projective Observation

