Abstract

This manuscript is a purely theoretical treatment of whether the public record can support inference about the effect of competitive pressure on the completion of pre-deployment safety evaluation in frontier AI. No empirical measurement, laboratory experiment, or computational training is undertaken. The object of study is the inferential architecture itself: what a selected, projectively observed record can and cannot identify, and what an auditable reporting instrument would have to look like given that structure. The central formal object is an observation map that records the product Z = MY of completion Y and disclosure M together with a provenance-annotated document lattice. Three impossibility results organise the theory. First, the completion probability is not identified on any class of data-generating processes that permits undisclosed completion, and a finite-sample minimax lower bound is proved for any estimator under the \Lambda-unbounded class. Second, because deployment timing is responsive to the same pressure that the hypothesis predicts affects completion, deployment censoring is informative rather than independent, and the observed conditional completion probability can reverse sign relative to the structural effect within a race-dominant class of release-timing models; a two-type construction establishes the reversal and delimits the class for which the structural sign is well-defined. Third, sharp partial-identification bounds are derived for the completion difference under a bounded-odds-of-nondisclosure model, with the zero-cell case stated explicitly and a closed-form breakdown quantity measuring the undisclosed mass required to reverse the sign. The admissibility rule is formalised as a closure operator on a lattice of provenance-graded documents, with the tier convention built into the definition rather than into the proof, yielding extensivity, monotonicity, and idempotence together with audit-stability corollaries. Inference relies on a stratified studentised permutation test whose exactness is proved for the sharp null on the observed outcomes and whose non-triviality requires an explicit orbit-size feasibility condition |\Omega(W)| \ge 1/\alpha. A frozen rival-set sensitivity decomposition reports fragility to the definition of the reference set. Impossibility theorems establish that the Blackwell order admits no scalar representation, that the structural release rule is not identified from timing marginals, and that the optimal review-window functional is unidentified without welfare parameters. A layered architecture with a determinism theorem and a record-monotonicity theorem guarantees that the report is a reproducible function of the frozen record and frozen parameters, and that adding documentation cannot silently overturn a passing audit. A constructive agenda ranks the minimal additional observables that would restore point identification, separating the necessary extensions from the sharpening ones. The design's primary expected outcome is an audit failure, which is reported as a measurement of the disclosure regime rather than as a procedural defect.

Keywords: partial identification; informative censoring; non-ignorable missingness; randomization inference; closure operators; identifiability; auditability; orbit feasibility.

---

1. Introduction

A recurring claim in AI governance asserts that competition between frontier developers compresses or weakens pre-deployment safety evaluation. The claim is theoretically underdetermined: formal models of release timing admit both a race to the bottom, in which first-mover advantage induces premature deployment, and insufficient entry, in which developers delay to free-ride on rivals' safety experimentation (Choi, Jeon, and Menicucci). The sign of the comparative static is a condition of the model rather than a theorem of it. This manuscript does not resolve the indeterminacy by assertion. It characterises, mathematically, the conditions under which any resolution is identifiable from the public record, and it proves that under natural assumptions those conditions fail.

The manuscript is a theory of inference, not an empirical study. Its primitives are a record, an observation map, a target functional, and a class of data-generating processes. Its results are non-identifiability theorems, sharp and outer partial-identification bounds, exactness and power results for a permutation procedure, impossibility theorems for excluded estimands, and correctness and complexity statements for algorithms that compute the identified sets. Empirical material is replaced by explicit assumptions, stated in Section 3 and labelled as premises rather than findings. Every theorem is stated with an explicit "under assumptions …" clause so that its dependence on the premises is immediate. Algorithms are given as mathematical specifications with correctness and complexity statements; no code is executed and no data are processed.

Two methodological choices distinguish this treatment from the measurement-design literature it responds to. First, the exposure is defined at evaluation start rather than at release, because a release-date definition conflates the pressure experienced during the evaluation with the length of the evaluation itself. Second, the deployment event is separated from the disclosure event: deployment is an observable censoring mechanism handled by conditioning, whereas disclosure is a non-observable missingness mechanism handled by partial identification. Conflating the two is the principal inferential error the theory is designed to prevent, and Lemma 1 makes the separation formal.

The central architectural commitment is that the record is a projection. It reveals a coarsening of the latent variables, not the latent variables themselves. The theory's contribution is to state precisely what can be recovered from the projection, what cannot, and what audit functionals are well-defined and reproducible regardless. The impossibility results are not obstacles to be overcome by better statistics; they are properties of the observation map. The partial-identification results are the appropriate positive objects once point identification fails. The audit is the appropriate primary object once the policy-relevant question is recast as a question about the disclosure regime rather than a question about a single structural parameter.

Relation to the companion protocol. The companion protocol (v0.3) implements the audit functional of Section 6 as an operational go/no-go rule. This manuscript establishes that the protocol's primary outcome — audit failure — is not a defect of the protocol but a property of the projective record under the current disclosure regime. The theory and the protocol together constitute a complete programme: the protocol measures the disclosure regime; the theory states why that measurement is the only well-posed empirical object. Throughout, a result is labelled conditional when it holds on a sub-class of data-generating processes, and unconditional when it holds on the whole class named in the theorem's hypothesis.

---

2. Notation and the Observation Map

Let \mathcal{R} denote a finite set of releases and \mathcal{D} a finite set of developers, with d(i) \in \mathcal{D} the developer of release i. For each i \in \mathcal{R}, let

s_i \in \mathbb{R}_{+} \quad \text{(evaluation start)}, \qquad
c_i \in (0, \infty] \quad \text{(completion time, absolute)}, \qquad
t_i^{B} \in \mathbb{R}_{+} \quad \text{(broad deployment)},

with \tau_i = c_i - s_i the evaluation duration, \tau_i = \infty permitted. The completion-before-deployment indicator is

Y_i = \mathbf{1}\{\tau_i < t_i^{B} - s_i\}.

Let M_i \in \{0,1\} indicate that the record contains a completion statement for i, and define the observed outcome

Z_i = M_i Y_i \in \{0,1\}.

The record reveals Z_i; it does not reveal M_i or Y_i separately. A release with Z_i = 0 is either incomplete or completed-but-undisclosed; the two are observationally equivalent.

For each release, let u_i denote a latent capacity type, \iota_i a latent independence regime, and a_i a latent access level. Let \text{docs}_i denote the provenance-annotated document set associated with i.

Definition 1 (Observation map). Let X_i = (s_i, \tau_i, u_i, \pi_i, \iota_i, a_i). The observation map \mathcal{O}: X \mapsto \mathcal{O}(X) is

\mathcal{O}(X) = \Big( \{t_i\}_{i \in \mathcal{R}},\ \{Z_i\}_{i \in \mathcal{A}},\ \{s_i, G_i\}_{i \in \mathcal{A}^{S}},\ \{\text{docs}_i\}_{i \in \mathcal{R}} \Big),

where \mathcal{A} \subseteq \mathcal{R} is the set of releases with an admitted deployment date and \mathcal{A}^{S} \subseteq \mathcal{A} is the subset with an admitted start date. The exposure G_i is defined by

G_i = s_i - \max\{ t_j : j \in \mathcal{R}_i,\ t_j \le s_i \}, \qquad t_j = s_j + \tau_j,

with \mathcal{R}_i \subseteq \mathcal{R} \setminus \{i\} the frozen rival set, and G_i = \bar{g} when the defining set is empty. All theorems concerning G are stated on the conditioning event i \in \mathcal{A}^{S}.

Remark 1 (Why the exposure is defined at evaluation start). A release-date definition \tilde{G}_i = t_i - \max\{t_j : t_j \le t_i\} is a special case of G_i that holds only if the start-map \sigma(\pi, u) is constant in pressure, an assumption the hypothesis itself contradicts. Under A3′, a longer evaluation pushes t_i later and mechanically enlarges \tilde{G}_i, so a release-date definition conflates the pressure experienced during the evaluation with the length of the evaluation itself. The start-defined G_i avoids this conflation; its cost is that G_i is observed only on \mathcal{A}^{S}, a limitation that is made explicit in every statement that uses it.

Lemma 1 (Separation of censoring and missingness). Under A1, the deployment event \{c_i \ge t_i^{B}\} is identified from the record whenever t_i^{B} is admitted, whereas the event \{Y_i = 1, M_i = 0\} is not identified for any release. Consequently, deployment must be handled by conditioning and disclosure must be handled by partial identification; neither can substitute for the other.

Proof. The first claim follows because t_i^{B} is recorded and c_i \ge t_i^{B} is equivalent to no completion statement preceding t_i^{B} together with a recorded deployment; the event is a measurable function of the record. The second claim follows because Z_i = 0 is consistent with both (Y_i, M_i) = (0, \cdot) and (Y_i, M_i) = (1, 0); no function of the record separates them. \square

Lemma 1 has an immediate consequence for coding: a release with no documented completion is missing, not censored. Coding it as censored imports the independence assumption of survival analysis into a setting where the hypothesis predicts that the censoring is informative.

