3. Assumptions and the Non-Emptiness of Regime Classes

No empirical measurement is performed in this manuscript. The following assumptions are stated as premises. Each is invoked explicitly by the theorem that requires it, and none is presented as a finding.

(A1) Projective record. The record reveals (Z_i, G_i, \text{covariates}_i) on \mathcal{A}^{S}; it does not reveal M_i or Y_i separately. On \mathcal{A} \setminus \mathcal{A}^{S}, the record reveals Z_i but neither s_i nor G_i.

(A2) Strategic disclosure. The probability of disclosure is non-increasing in latent pressure: P(M = 1 \mid Y = 1, \pi) is non-increasing in \pi. This is a premise, not an estimate.

(A3′) Race-dominant regime (conditional). Let \mathcal{M}_{\text{race}} \subset \mathcal{M} be the subclass of release-timing models in which the race equilibrium dominates, so that the deployment map is non-increasing in pressure: t^{B}(\pi) is non-increasing in \pi for each u. Within \mathcal{M}_{\text{race}}, the evaluation duration f(\pi, u) is strictly decreasing in \pi. All results concerning the structural sign are stated for P \in \mathcal{P}(\mathcal{M}_{\text{race}}). The complementary class \mathcal{M}_{\text{free}} is retained in the theoretical landscape, and the question of which class the data-generating process lies in is unverifiable from the record. A3′ is a conditioning event, not a universal claim.

(A4) Theoretical indeterminacy. The release-timing model \mathcal{M} admits both the race equilibrium of \mathcal{M}_{\text{race}} and a free-riding equilibrium in \mathcal{M}_{\text{free}}, so that the sign of the comparative static is not determined by the model alone.

(A5) Bounded undisclosed odds. There exists \Lambda \in [0, \infty) such that, for each g \in \{\text{lo}, \text{hi}\}, the odds of completed-but-undisclosed to disclosed within group g is bounded:

\lambda_g = \frac{P(M = 0, Y = 1 \mid G \in \mathcal{G}_g)}{P(M = 1, Y = 1 \mid G \in \mathcal{G}_g)} \le \Lambda.

The parameter \Lambda indexes the identified set and is treated as a sensitivity parameter, not as an estimated quantity.

(A6) Frozen rival set. \mathcal{R}_i is fixed at a freeze date and is not revised in response to the data.

(A7) Frozen thresholds and parameters. The analysis parameters \Theta = (N_{\text{adm}}^*, N_{\text{labs}}^*, p_{\text{indep}}^*, g_0, \bar{g}, \Lambda, B, \alpha) are frozen after instrument calibration and before the hypothesis comparison; they are not revised thereafter in a way that depends on the observed Z.

(A8) Independent strata. Releases by different developers are independent conditional on covariates; releases by the same developer are exchangeable within strata under the sharp null.

Assumptions A1 and A2 jointly generate the non-identifiability results of Section 4. Assumption A3′ with A4 generates the sign-reversal result of Section 4. Assumptions A5–A7 generate the auditability results of Sections 6 and 11.

Lemma 2 (Non-emptiness of the regime classes). Under A4, both \mathcal{M}_{\text{race}} and \mathcal{M}_{\text{free}} are non-empty.

Proof. It suffices to exhibit a two-type model in the primitives of Section 2 with both signs of the comparative static. Let u \in \{H, L\} with capacity H > L. Let the deployment map be

t^{B}(\pi, u) = \bar{t} - \beta_u \pi + \varepsilon, \qquad \beta_H > 0,\ \beta_L < 0,

with \varepsilon a mean-zero shock. For a population concentrated on type H, t^{B} is non-increasing in \pi for every u in the support, so the resulting model belongs to \mathcal{M}_{\text{race}}. For a population concentrated on type L, t^{B} is increasing in \pi, so the resulting model belongs to \mathcal{M}_{\text{free}}. Both are mixtures over the same latent types and satisfy the primitives; hence both classes are non-empty. \square

Lemma 2 converts the status of A3′ from an assumption that smuggles the conclusion into a conditioning event whose complement is a coherent, non-empty alternative. The paper's structural-sign results are henceforth explicitly conditional on P \in \mathcal{P}(\mathcal{M}_{\text{race}}), and the unverifiability of the class membership is stated as a property of the record rather than as a defect of the theory.

