4. Non-Identifiability

Throughout this section, statements are conditioned on i \in \mathcal{A}^{S} whenever they involve G. For notational economy, "G \in \mathcal{G}" abbreviates "i \in \mathcal{A}^{S} with G_i \in \mathcal{G}." We write L for the low-pressure group \mathcal{G}_{\text{lo}} and H for the high-pressure group \mathcal{G}_{\text{hi}}, with \Delta = \pi_L - \pi_H.

Theorem 1 (Non-identifiability of the completion probability). Let \mathcal{P} be the class of data-generating processes satisfying A1 and A2 with G observed on \mathcal{A}^{S}. The functional P \mapsto P(Y = 1 \mid G \in \mathcal{G}) is not identified on \mathcal{P}.

Proof. Fix P \in \mathcal{P} and let p = P(Z = 1 \mid G \in \mathcal{G}). Construct P^{(1)} with P^{(1)}(M = 1 \mid Y = 1, G \in \mathcal{G}) = 1, so that P^{(1)}(Y = 1 \mid G \in \mathcal{G}) = p. Construct P^{(2)} with P^{(2)}(M = 1 \mid Y = 1, G \in \mathcal{G}) = q \in (0,1) and P^{(2)}(Y = 1 \mid G \in \mathcal{G}) = p/q \le 1. Then P^{(2)}(Z = 1 \mid G \in \mathcal{G}) = p as well, so P^{(1)} and P^{(2)} induce the same observed law on (Z, G). Their completion probabilities differ by p(1/q - 1) > 0. Since the observed law is the only datum, no functional of it equals P(Y = 1 \mid G \in \mathcal{G}) on both. \square

Theorem 2 (Minimax lower bound, finite-sample version). Let \mathcal{P} denote the class of data-generating processes satisfying A1 and A2 with the observed law P_{\text{obs}} on (Z, G) fixed and with p_g = P_{\text{obs}}(Z = 1 \mid G \in \mathcal{G}_g). For any estimator \hat\Delta of the group difference \Delta = \pi_L - \pi_H,

\sup_{P \in \mathcal{P}(P_{\text{obs}})} \mathbb{E}_P\big[(\hat\Delta - \Delta(P))^2\big] \;\ge\; \frac{1}{4}\max_{g \in \{L, H\}} (1 - p_g)^2.

Proof. Assume p_H < 1, without loss; the argument for p_L < 1 is symmetric. Construct P_1 \in \mathcal{P}(P_{\text{obs}}) with full disclosure in both groups and completion probabilities \pi_L = p_L, \pi_H = p_H, so that \Delta(P_1) = p_L - p_H. Construct P_2 \in \mathcal{P}(P_{\text{obs}}) with full completion in the high-pressure group and disclosure probability \delta_H = p_H, and full completion and full disclosure in the low-pressure group. Then \pi_L = p_L and \pi_H = 1, so \Delta(P_2) = p_L - 1. The disclosure ordering required by A2, \delta_H \le \delta_L, is satisfied because \delta_H = p_H \le 1 = \delta_L. Both P_1 and P_2 induce P_{\text{obs}} on the marginals. Since \hat\Delta is a function of the observed law, \hat\Delta(P_1) = \hat\Delta(P_2) =: \hat\Delta_0. Then

\max_{k\in\{1,2\}} (\hat\Delta_0 - \Delta(P_k))^2 \;\ge\; \Big(\tfrac{\Delta(P_2) - \Delta(P_1)}{2}\Big)^2 \;=\; \frac{(1 - p_H)^2}{4}.

The supremum over \mathcal{P}(P_{\text{obs}}) dominates the maximum. Repeating with the roles of L and H interchanged (using p_L < 1) yields the stated maximum. When both p_g = 1, the identified set is a point and the bound is vacuous, which is consistent with the claim. \square

Remark 2 (Index convention). The construction in Theorem 2 places the full-completion, low-disclosure regime in the high-pressure group. This is the only placement consistent with A2, which requires disclosure to be non-increasing in pressure. Placing the low-disclosure regime in the low-pressure group would violate the monotonicity of A2 and is therefore inadmissible.

Theorem 3 (Sign reversal under informative censoring, race-dominant class). Suppose A3′ holds and P \in \mathcal{P}(\mathcal{M}_{\text{race}}), so that the structural completion probability is decreasing in pressure. Under A1, the observed conditional probability g \mapsto P(Y = 1 \mid G = g) need not be decreasing in g; it may be increasing on an interval, constant, or non-monotone, even when the structural effect has a single sign everywhere.

Proof. Let the latent type u \in \{A, B\} be drawn with P(u = A \mid G = g) = \mu(g), determined by the rival schedule and by the type-specific release policy rather than by the causal effect. Let the type-conditional completion probabilities be \alpha_A(g) and \alpha_B(g), both strictly decreasing in g by A3′. The observed conditional is

P(Y = 1 \mid G = g) = \mu(g)\,\alpha_A(g) + \big(1 - \mu(g)\big)\,\alpha_B(g).

Differentiating with respect to g,

\frac{d}{dg} P(Y = 1 \mid G = g) = \mu'(g)\big(\alpha_A(g) - \alpha_B(g)\big) + \mu(g)\,\alpha_A'(g) + \big(1-\mu(g)\big)\,\alpha_B'(g).

The last two terms are negative by A3′. The first term is positive whenever \mu'(g) > 0 and \alpha_A(g) > \alpha_B(g), that is, whenever the composition of types shifts toward the higher-capacity type as the gap widens, and the shift is large enough. Choosing \mu with sufficiently large positive slope on an interval yields a positive observed gradient on that interval. Since \mu is not a function of the causal effect, the observed sign is not informative about the structural sign. \square

Corollary 1. The descriptive functional \Delta does not identify the structural sign. Reporting \hat\Delta as evidence for or against the hypothesis in the structural sense is invalid; it is valid only as a description of the observed conditional.

Corollary 2. A gap to a release outside the frozen rival set does not adjudicate the hypothesis, because the type-composition mechanism of Theorem 3 operates for any reference release. The negative-control exposure is at most a descriptive diagnostic of the confounding structure, not a falsification device.

Theorems 1–3 establish that the object of inference cannot be a point-identified structural parameter. It must be either the identified set (Section 5) or the audit functional (Section 6), and the descriptive \Delta can only be reported alongside its identified set and its breakdown quantity.

