5. Partial Identification

Definition 2 (Identified set). For a class \mathcal{P}(\Lambda) of data-generating processes satisfying A1, A2 and A5, and for a functional \theta: \mathcal{P} \to \mathbb{R}, the identified set is

\Theta(\Lambda) = \big\{ \theta(P) : P \in \mathcal{P}(\Lambda),\ P \text{ induces the observed law on } (Z, G) \big\}.

For the group-specific completion probability, write

p_g = P(Z = 1 \mid G \in \mathcal{G}_g), \qquad \pi_g = P(Y = 1 \mid G \in \mathcal{G}_g).

Theorem 4 (Sharp group bounds, with the zero-cell case). Under A1, A2 and A5, the sharp identified set for \pi_g is

\Pi_g(\Lambda) =
\begin{cases}
\{0\}, & p_g = 0 \text{ and } \Lambda < \infty,\\[4pt]
[0, 1], & p_g = 0 \text{ and } \Lambda = \infty,\\[4pt]
\big[\, p_g,\ \min\{1,\ p_g(1+\Lambda)\} \,\big], & p_g > 0.
\end{cases}

Proof. Consider the case p_g > 0. By A5, the undisclosed mass is a_g = \pi_g - p_g \ge 0 with a_g / p_g = \lambda_g \le \Lambda, so \pi_g \le p_g(1+\Lambda); also \pi_g \le 1 since it is a probability, and \pi_g \ge p_g since a_g \ge 0. For sharpness, fix any \pi \in [p_g, \min\{1, p_g(1+\Lambda)\}] and set

P(Y = 1, M = 1 \mid \mathcal{G}_g) = p_g, \quad
P(Y = 1, M = 0 \mid \mathcal{G}_g) = \pi - p_g, \quad
P(Y = 0 \mid \mathcal{G}_g) = 1 - \pi.

All three quantities are non-negative by the constraints on \pi, and they sum to one. The induced P(Z = 1 \mid \mathcal{G}_g) = p_g, and the implied odds ratio is (\pi - p_g)/p_g \le \Lambda. Hence \pi is attained. Consider the case p_g = 0. If \Lambda < \infty, any completed-but-undisclosed mass a_g > 0 would produce odds \lambda_g = a_g / 0 = +\infty, violating A5; hence a_g = 0 and \pi_g = 0. If \Lambda = \infty, the odds constraint imposes no restriction, and \pi_g is unrestricted on [0,1]. \square

Corollary 3 (Minkowski-difference bounds on the difference). Under the assumptions of Theorem 4 with p_L, p_H > 0, the set

\Delta \in \Big[\, p_L - \min\{1, p_H(1+\Lambda)\},\ \min\{1, p_L(1+\Lambda)\} - p_H \,\Big]

contains the identified set for \Delta on any class satisfying A1, A2 and A5. The containment is sharp under A8 (independence of groups); under a shared dependence structure that constrains the joint law of (Z_L, Z_H) beyond the marginals, the sharper bound is obtained by solving the linear programme in Proposition 1.

Proof. The bounds are the Minkowski difference of the two group identified sets and therefore contain the identified set of the difference for any dependence structure consistent with the marginals. Sharpness under independence follows because the separability of the group constraints means every pair (\pi_L, \pi_H) \in \Pi_L(\Lambda) \times \Pi_H(\Lambda) is attainable, and the Minkowski difference is the image of the product set under the map (\pi_L, \pi_H) \mapsto \pi_L - \pi_H. \square

Proposition 1 (Joint-law sharp bound). Suppose the observed joint law of (Z_L, Z_H) is fixed, and let \kappa = P(Z_L = 1, Z_H = 1) be the observed co-occurrence. The sharp identified set for \Delta is the value set of the linear programme

\min / \max\ \Delta = \pi_L - \pi_H \quad \text{s.t.}\quad
\sum_{(M_L, Y_L)} \!\! P(M_L, Y_L, M_H, Y_H) = \text{target marginals,}

subject to the odds constraints P(M_g = 0, Y_g = 1) \le \Lambda\, P(M_g = 1, Y_g = 1) and the observation constraints P(M_g = 1, Y_g = 1) = p_g for each g, together with the joint feasibility constraints that any shared dependence structure imposes on the four-way table.

Proof. The identification region is the image of a polytope defined by linear equalities and inequalities, and linear functionals attain extrema at vertices of polytopes; the sharp bounds are those vertex values. \square

When A8 is maintained, the joint feasibility constraint reduces to factorization and the polytope's value set coincides with the Minkowski difference of Corollary 3. The proposition is included so that the sharp bound under dependence is stated and not merely gestured at.

Definition 3 (Breakdown bound). The breakdown bound is

\Lambda^{*} = \inf\big\{ \Lambda \ge 0 : \underline{\Delta}(\Lambda) \le 0 \le \overline{\Delta}(\Lambda) \big\},

where \underline{\Delta}, \overline{\Delta} are the endpoints of Corollary 3. \Lambda^{*} is the quantity reported in place of a significance test.

Proposition 2 (Closed form for the breakdown bound). Suppose p_L, p_H \in (0,1). Then

\Lambda^{*} = \max\!\left\{0,\ \frac{p_L}{p_H} - 1,\ \frac{p_H}{p_L} - 1\right\} = \frac{|p_L - p_H|}{\min\{p_L, p_H\}}.

If p_L = 0 or p_H = 0, then \Lambda^{*} = \infty.

Proof. The lower endpoint satisfies \underline{\Delta}(\Lambda) \le 0 iff p_L \le \min\{1, p_H(1+\Lambda)\}, which holds once p_H(1+\Lambda) \ge p_L, i.e. \Lambda \ge p_L/p_H - 1. The upper endpoint satisfies \overline{\Delta}(\Lambda) \ge 0 iff \min\{1, p_L(1+\Lambda)\} \ge p_H, which holds once p_L(1+\Lambda) \ge p_H, i.e. \Lambda \ge p_H/p_L - 1. Taking the larger of the two thresholds gives the stated formula. If one of p_L, p_H is zero, one of the thresholds is infinite, so no finite \Lambda yields an interval containing zero. \square

Proposition 3 (Monotone-selection refinement). Suppose in addition that the disclosure odds are ordered across groups, \lambda_H \le \lambda_L. Then the identified set is contained in the set obtained by imposing \lambda_H = \lambda_L = \lambda \le \Lambda, and the difference bounds tighten to

\Delta \in \Big[\, p_L(1+\lambda^{*}) - p_H(1+\Lambda),\ p_L(1+\Lambda) - p_H(1+\lambda^{*}) \,\Big]

with \lambda^{*} = \max\{0, p_H^{-1} - 1\} the smallest feasible common odds consistent with the observed p_H.

Proof. The equality case follows by solving the two equations p_g(1+\lambda) = \pi_g for a common \lambda subject to \pi_g \le 1. The inequality case follows since \lambda_H \le \lambda_L \le \Lambda restricts the pair (\lambda_H, \lambda_L) to a simplex whose extreme points generate the stated interval. \square

Proposition 3 is the formal counterpart of Lee-type trimming. It is stated as a proposition rather than a theorem because the ordering assumption is substantive and not testable from the record.

Remark 3 (Percentile imputation is not a bound). Imputing the missing outcomes at empirical percentiles of the observed distribution produces neither a sharp bound nor a valid confidence set; it is a sensitivity exercise whose output depends on the percentile chosen. The support bounds of Theorem 4 are sharp and assumption-explicit; the percentile exercise may be reported only under the label sensitivity analysis.

5.1 Rival-set sensitivity and the fragility index

The freeze in A6 is required for determinism (Theorem 11) but is not an identification assumption. The exposure depends on which releases are admitted as rivals, and a definition that excludes a materially relevant release can shift G_i without changing the data-generating process.

Definition 4 (Frozen and dynamic exposures). Let \mathcal{R}_i^{\text{frozen}} be the rival set fixed at the freeze date, and let \mathcal{R}_i^{\text{dyn}}(h) = \{j \neq i : |t_j - t_i| \le h\} be the dynamic rival set within window h. The corresponding exposures are

G_i^{\text{frozen}} = s_i - \max\{t_j : j \in \mathcal{R}_i^{\text{frozen}},\ t_j \le s_i\},
\qquad
G_i^{\text{dyn}}(h) = s_i - \max\{t_j : j \in \mathcal{R}_i^{\text{dyn}}(h),\ t_j \le s_i\}.

Definition 5 (Fragility index). Let \Theta^{\text{frozen}}(\Lambda) and \Theta^{\text{dyn}}(\Lambda; h) be the identified sets of Corollary 3 computed under the two exposures. The fragility index is

\Phi(h) = \mathbf{1}\Big\{ \operatorname{sign}\big(\overline{\Delta}^{\text{frozen}} - \underline{\Delta}^{\text{frozen}}\big) \neq \operatorname{sign}\big(\overline{\Delta}^{\text{dyn}} - \underline{\Delta}^{\text{dyn}}\big) \Big\} \;\vee\; \mathbf{1}\big\{ \Lambda^{*,\text{frozen}} \neq \Lambda^{*,\text{dyn}} \big\}.

\Phi(h) = 1 means the inferential content of the record depends on the rival-set definition; the finding is then reported as fragile to rival-set definition, which is a statement about the exposure measurement rather than about the hypothesis. The decomposition is descriptive and is not assigned a p-value.

