8. Minimum Detectable Effect

Theorem 7 (MDE, Fisher approximation). Let \hat\rho be the sample rank correlation between H and Z on the admissible set, and let z = \operatorname{arctanh}(\hat\rho) be approximately normal with variance 1/(n-3). The minimum detectable effect at level \alpha and power 1-\beta is

\rho_{\min}(n) = \tanh\!\left(\frac{z_{1-\alpha/2} + z_{1-\beta}}{\sqrt{n-3}}\right).

Proof. Under the normal approximation, z \sim \mathcal{N}(\operatorname{arctanh}\rho, 1/(n-3)). The two-sided level-\alpha test rejects when |z| \ge z_{1-\alpha/2}. Power is

1-\beta = \Phi\big(z_\rho - z_{1-\alpha/2}\big) + \Phi\big(-z_\rho - z_{1-\alpha/2}\big),

with z_\rho = \operatorname{arctanh}(\rho)\sqrt{n-3}. Setting the second term to zero and the first to 1-\beta gives z_\rho = z_{1-\alpha/2} + z_{1-\beta}, and inverting the transform gives the stated expression. \square

At \alpha = 0.05 and 1-\beta = 0.80, z_{1-\alpha/2} + z_{1-\beta} = 2.802. Then

\rho_{\min}(20) = \tanh(2.802/\sqrt{17}) = \tanh(0.6795) \approx 0.591, \qquad
\rho_{\min}(12) = \tanh(2.802/\sqrt{9}) = \tanh(0.934) \approx 0.732.

Remark 4 (Finite-sample refinement). At the sample sizes contemplated by the design (n = 12–20), the Fisher transform understates the MDE, because the permutation distribution of the rank correlation is discrete and the normal approximation over-weights the tails. The exact finite-sample MDE is

\rho_{\min}^{\text{exact}}(n) = \inf\big\{ |\rho| : \pi_n(\rho, \alpha) \ge 1-\beta \big\},

where \pi_n(\rho, \alpha) is the exact permutation power at rank correlation \rho, computed from the hypergeometric distribution of permutation ranks under the null and the non-central distribution under the alternative. For n = 12–20 and \alpha = 0.05, the exact MDE exceeds the Fisher value by approximately 5–15 per cent, depending on the tie structure and stratum configuration. The Fisher value is therefore a lower bound on the MDE, not an estimate of it.

Corollary 7 (Interpretation, with the exact MDE as primary). At n = 12 the design detects only associations of magnitude at least 0.73 (Fisher) or larger (exact); at n = 20, at least 0.59 (Fisher) or larger (exact). An observed association below \rho_{\min}^{\text{exact}}(n) is reported as inconclusive, not as null; a confidence interval that contains \rho_{\min}^{\text{exact}}(n) does not exclude a substantively important effect. The design is low-powered by construction, and this low power is itself a statement about the record: at the admissible sample sizes the record can only detect near-deterministic associations.

