11. Architecture and Determinism

The design is a layered deterministic pipeline. Each layer is a map on the lattice of states of the layer below.

· Layer 0 (Record). An immutable, versioned document set S. Edits are new versions; the layer is a partially ordered set under version extension.
· Layer 1 (Provenance). The closure \mathcal{A}_T computed by Algorithm 1. By Theorem 5 this layer is a closure operator on Layer 0.
· Layer 2 (Admissibility). The sets \mathcal{A}^{\text{strict}}, \mathcal{A}^{\text{trip}}, and the audit quantities. By Corollary 4 this layer is monotone in Layer 1.
· Layer 3 (Partial identification). The sets \Pi_g(\Lambda), the \Delta interval, the joint-law sharp bound, \Lambda^{*}, and the fragility index, computed by Algorithms 2, 3 and 7 from Layer 2.
· Layer 4 (Inference). The orbit feasibility flag, the permutation p-value, and the confidence set from Algorithm 4, computed from Layer 2 and the frozen parameters.
· Layer 5 (Report). A deterministic rendering of Layers 2–4 and the frozen parameters.

Theorem 11 (Determinism). The report is a deterministic function of the frozen record S_{t_f} and the frozen parameters \Theta_{t_f}. Two analysts equipped with the same (S_{t_f}, \Theta_{t_f}) produce identical reports.

Proof. Layers 1–4 are deterministic maps by Algorithms 1–4 and 7 and Definition 8; Layer 5 is deterministic by construction. The composition of deterministic maps is deterministic. \square

Theorem 12 (Record monotonicity of the audit). If S_{t_f} \subseteq S'_{t_f} and the frozen parameters are unchanged, then N_{\text{adm}}(S') \ge N_{\text{adm}}(S_{t_f}) and the threshold decision cannot change from pass to fail.

Proof. Corollary 4 applied to Layer 2; the decision is a monotone function of the audit quantities by construction. \square

