12. Discussion

12.1 What the theory establishes and what it does not

The theory delivers a specific verdict on the inferential question. The completion probability is not identified from the public record under any class of data-generating processes that permits undisclosed completion (Theorem 1), a finite-sample minimax lower bound holds for any estimator on the \Lambda-unbounded class (Theorem 2), and the observed conditional probability can reverse sign relative to the structural effect within the race-dominant class (Theorem 3). These are properties of the observation map, not obstacles that better statistics could remove. The only defensible inferential objects are the identified sets of Section 5, the breakdown bound \Lambda^{*}, and the audit quantities of Section 6.

Three consequences follow. First, the design's primary estimand is the audit functional, not the completion difference. The audit is a deterministic, monotone, reproducible function of the record (Theorems 11 and 12), and its failure is a measurement of the disclosure regime rather than a defect of the study. Second, the descriptive completion difference is reported only as a description, with bounds and a breakdown quantity, and never as evidence for or against the structural hypothesis (Corollary 6). Third, the survival model is retained only as an exploratory summary, because its independent-censoring assumption is contradicted by the hypothesis itself (Lemma 1 and Theorem 3).

12.2 The role of \Lambda

Assumption A5 bounds the undisclosed odds, and the breakdown bound \Lambda^{*} reports how much undisclosed mass would suffice to reverse the sign. The programme does not pretend to estimate \Lambda from the record, because Theorem 2 forbids it. It reports \Lambda^{*} and leaves the judgment of whether \Lambda^{*} is small or large to the reader, with the explicit statement that this judgment is substantive and not statistical. Two reference values may be supplied as sensitivity benchmarks — the silent-revision rates reported in the safety-framework literature, and a simple upper bound derived from the maximum plausible fraction of completed-but-undisclosed evaluations consistent with the observed deployment behaviour — but both are calibrated to adjacent rather than to the same object, and the mapping between them and \Lambda is an assumption. They are reported alongside \Lambda^{*} with the assumption stated, not as calibrations of \Lambda. The zero-cell result (Theorem 4) makes this concrete: under the observed disclosure regime, p_g = 0 in the high-pressure group is plausible, and then \Lambda^{*} = \infty for any finite \Lambda, so the sign is unidentified even at the trivial bound.

12.3 The theoretical indeterminacy is not resolved by the design

Theorems 1–3 do not resolve the Choi–Jeon–Menicucci indeterminacy between race and free-riding equilibria. Lemma 2 establishes that both classes are non-empty under the primitives, and A3′ is stated as a conditioning event rather than a universal claim. The behavioural literature on AI races, which finds that falling behind increases unsafe play in a laboratory setting, motivates the race-dominant class A3′ as a plausible maintained hypothesis. It does not identify it. The design is honest about this: the structural sign is stated as a hypothesis, the observed sign is treated as descriptive, and the gap between them is a partial-identification interval whose width is measured by \Lambda^{*}.

12.4 The impossibility results delimit the programme

The Blackwell order admits no scalar representation (Theorem 8), so no composite informativeness index is estimable from documents that do not record evaluator posteriors. The structural release rule is not identified from timing marginals (Theorem 9), so structural inversion is unavailable. The optimal review window is unidentified without welfare parameters (Theorem 10), so the policy anchors are used only descriptively. These exclusions are theorems, not conveniences, and they should be treated as boundaries of the research programme rather than as gaps to be filled by future work in the same direction.

12.5 The audit functional as primary object

The primary scientific contribution is the triple (N_{\text{adm}}, N_{\text{labs}}, p_{\text{indep}}) together with the disclosure-gap report — the histogram of missing fields, the fraction of releases lacking an admitted start–deployment pair, the orbit feasibility flag, the exact MDE, and the list of regime changes that would close the gap. These quantities are deterministic, monotone in the record, reproducible under the freeze operator, and independent of the hypothesis-testing apparatus. They answer a well-posed question: can the public record support inference about the competition–safety relationship at the stated standard? The answer is likely no, and reporting that answer with the same prominence a hypothesis test would receive is the design's substantive contribution. The partial-identification bounds on \Delta and the fragility index are secondary descriptive objects whose interpretation is deliberately limited by Theorems 1–3.

12.6 Relation to the companion protocol

The companion protocol (v0.3) is the measurement protocol that this theory proves cannot achieve point identification. Its go/no-go rule is an instantiation of the audit functional of Section 6. The theory and the protocol are not competing papers: the protocol measures the disclosure regime; the theory states why that measurement is the only well-posed empirical object under projective observation. Read together, they constitute a complete programme: theory of the observation map, instrument for the audit, and a constructive specification for the disclosure regime that would restore identification.

---

13. Constructive Agenda: Minimal Additional Observables

The impossibility results of Sections 4 and 9 are not verdicts on the hypothesis; they are statements about what the record would need to contain for the hypothesis to become estimable. The following additions to the observation map are ranked by what they restore. The ranking separates the minimal extensions, without which point identification is impossible, from the sharpening extensions, which tighten the identified set or the dependence of the bounds on the disclosure regime.

(C1) Mandatory disclosure of completion status (minimal). If a completion statement is required for every evaluation that was initiated, whether or not it was published, then M_i \equiv 1 on the admitted set and the projection Z = MY collapses to Y. Theorems 1 and 2 become vacuous, \Pi_g(\Lambda) collapses to a point, and the permutation test of Section 7 acquires power against the structural hypothesis through the observed Y. The requirement is strictly on the existence of the completion statement, not on its content, so it does not force disclosure of evaluator conclusions.

(C3) Standardised reporting of evaluation start dates (minimal). If s_i is reported for every evaluation, the conditioning event \mathcal{A}^{S} expands to \mathcal{A} and the theorems of Section 4 apply to the full admissible set rather than to the subset with documented start dates. The current theory restricts itself to \mathcal{A}^{S} because G_i is otherwise unobservable; standardised start-date reporting removes the restriction.

(C2) Contractual independence of publication rights from pressure (sharpening). If the independence regime \iota_i is fixed at the outset of the evaluation and is not adjustable in response to competitive conditions, then the regime stratifier is exogenous to the exposure and the partial-identification bounds sharpen. The weaker requirement, sufficient for the design as stated, is that any change to \iota_i be dated and disclosed. The stronger requirement would make the regime a pre-determined covariate and permit a version of Theorem 3 in which the type-composition mechanism operates only through capacity.

(C4) Bounded \Lambda via transparency requirements (sharpening). If disclosure is required to be audited — for example, by an independent body that certifies the fraction of completed evaluations that were published — then \Lambda is bounded above by the inverse of the certified disclosure fraction, the identified set of Theorem 4 sharpens, and the breakdown bound \Lambda^{*} becomes interpretable as a policy-relevant quantity: the level of undisclosed mass above which the sign is not determined by the data. The certifier must itself be outside the pressure channel; otherwise the bound is circular.

(C5) Enumeration duty on safety-framework revisions (sharpening). If every material revision of a safety framework must enumerate what changed — not why — then the silent-revision rate is directly observable and supplies a reference value for the adjacent-object calibration of \Lambda. The requirement is strictly weaker than a justification duty and is compatible with trade-secret protection of the underlying rationale.

(C6) Standardised rival-set disclosure (sharpening). If the rival set used in the exposure definition is reported and justified by the developer or by the auditor, the fragility index \Phi(h) becomes interpretable as a property of the disclosure regime rather than of the analyst's choice, and the freeze in A6 becomes a documented convention rather than a hidden assumption.

Table 1. Priority ranking of the constructive extensions.

Extension What it restores Priority
C1 Mandatory completion statement Collapses Z = MY to Y; removes Theorems 1–2 Minimal
C3 Standardised start dates Expands \mathcal{A}^{S} to \mathcal{A}; makes all G-theorems apply to the full admissible set Minimal
C2 Independence fixed at outset Tightens type-composition mechanism in Theorem 3 Sharpening
C4 Audited disclosure fraction Bounds \Lambda; sharpens Theorem 4 Sharpening
C5 Enumeration duty on revisions Supplies adjacent-object calibration for \Lambda Sharpening
C6 Standardised rival-set disclosure Makes the fragility index interpretable; documents A6 Sharpening

The table converts the agenda from a wish list into a specification with an implementation order. A governance mechanism that adopts C1 and C3 alone would make the question of Section 1 estimable in the strong sense; the other four improve the precision and the interpretability of the answer without being necessary for point identification.

---

14. Conclusion

The manuscript has developed a theory of inference for a question that the public record cannot answer in its current form. The projection Z = MY discards the distinction between incomplete and completed-but-undisclosed evaluations; no statistical procedure can recover what the projection discards (Theorems 1–2); and the observed conditional probability can reverse sign relative to the structural effect even within the class where that effect is well-defined (Theorem 3, with Lemma 2 establishing that the conditioning class is non-empty and that its complement is coherent). The positive objects are the sharp partial-identification bounds of Theorem 4, with the zero-cell case stated explicitly and the breakdown bound of Proposition 2 given in closed form; the audit algebra of Section 6, whose closure-operator structure guarantees that adding documentation cannot overturn a passing audit; the stratified permutation test of Section 7, whose exactness is stated on the observed outcomes and whose non-triviality requires an explicit orbit-size condition; the exact-MDE interpretation of Section 8; and the determinism and record-monotonicity theorems of Section 11, which make the report a reproducible function of the frozen inputs.

The primary expected outcome is an audit failure, reported as a measurement of the disclosure regime rather than as a procedural defect. The constructive agenda ranks the minimal observables that would restore point identification and separates them from the sharpening extensions. The companion protocol implements the audit functional operationally; the theory states why that functional is the only well-posed empirical object under projective observation. The public record, as currently constituted, cannot answer the question that policy discourse treats as settled; the theory states what the record would need to contain for the question to become answerable, in the same formal language as the impossibility results, so that the two can be compared directly. That comparison is the programme's ultimate contribution.

