Appendix B. Proofs for Sections 2–4

B.1 Lemma 1 (Separation of censoring and missingness)

Under A1, the deployment event is identified whenever $t_i^B$ is admitted, whereas $\{Y_i=1,M_i=0\}$ is not identified for any release.

Proof. $Z_i=1$ forces $M_i=1$ and $Y_i=1$. $Z_i=0$ is consistent with both $(Y_i,M_i)=(0,\cdot)$ and $(1,0)$, since $0=M\cdot 0$ and $0=0\cdot 1$. Because $Z_i$ is the only observation on $(M_i,Y_i)$, no functional of the record separates the two. $\square$

Consequence. A release with $Z_i=0$ is missing, not censored. Coding it as censored imports $M_i\equiv 1$ on $\{Y_i=1\}$ — the exact identification assumption the hypothesis predicts fails in the high-pressure group.

B.2 Lemma 2 (Non-emptiness of the regime classes)

Under A4, both $\mathcal{M}_{\mathrm{race}}$ and $\mathcal{M}_{\mathrm{free}}$ are non-empty.

Proof. Take a two-type model $u\in\{H,L\}$ with $t^B(\pi,u)=\bar{t}-\beta_u\pi+\varepsilon$, $\varepsilon$ mean-zero, $\beta_H>0$, $\beta_L<0$, and duration $f(\pi,u)=\bar{f}-\gamma\pi$, $\gamma>0$. A population concentrated on $H$ yields $t^B$ strictly decreasing and $f$ strictly decreasing in $\pi$, hence a member of $\mathcal{M}_{\mathrm{race}}$ satisfying A3′. A population concentrated on $L$ yields $t^B$ increasing in $\pi$, hence a member of $\mathcal{M}_{\mathrm{free}}$. Both satisfy the primitives of Section 2. $\square$

A3′ is therefore a conditioning event $P\in\mathcal{P}(\mathcal{M}_{\mathrm{race}})$, not a universal claim, and its complement is coherent.

B.3 Theorem 1 (Non-identifiability of the completion probability)

The functional $P\mapsto P(Y=1\mid G\in\mathcal{G})$ is not identified on the class satisfying A1 and A2.

Proof. Fix $p=P(Z=1\mid\mathcal{G})\in(0,1)$. Let $P^{(1)}$ have $P^{(1)}(M=1\mid Y=1)=1$ and $P^{(1)}(Y=1)=p$, so $P^{(1)}(Z=1)=p$. Fix $q\in[p,1)$ and let $P^{(2)}$ have $P^{(2)}(Y=1)=p/q$ and $P^{(2)}(M=1\mid Y=1)=q$, so $P^{(2)}(Z=1)=p$. Both induce the same observed law while $P^{(1)}(Y=1)-P^{(2)}(Y=1)=p(1-1/q)\neq 0$. At $p=0$, $P^{(1)}(Y=1)=0$ and $P^{(2)}(Y=1)=1$ with $M\equiv 0$ both give $P(Z=1)=0$; at $p=1$, $Y\equiv 1$ and $M\equiv 1$ are forced and identification holds at the boundary. $\square$

B.4 Theorem 2 (Finite-sample minimax lower bound)

Fix $P_{\mathrm{obs}}$ on $(Z,G)$ with $p_g=P_{\mathrm{obs}}(Z=1\mid\mathcal{G}_g)$. For any estimator $\hat\Delta$ of $\Delta$,
\sup_{P\in\mathcal{P}(P_{\mathrm{obs}})}\mathbb{E}_P[(\hat\Delta-\Delta(P))^2]\ge \frac14\max_{g\in\{L,H\}}(1-p_g)^2.

Proof. Assume $p_H<1$ without loss. Under A2, disclosure is non-increasing in pressure, so a lower-disclosure regime must sit in $H$. Construct $P_1$ with full disclosure $\delta_L=\delta_H=1$ and $\pi_g^{(1)}=p_g$; then $\Delta(P_1)=p_L-p_H$. Construct $P_2$ with full disclosure in $L$ and full completion in $H$ with disclosure $\delta_H=p_H$, so $\pi_L^{(2)}=p_L$, $\pi_H^{(2)}=1$, and $\Delta(P_2)=p_L-1$. A2 holds because $\delta_H=p_H\le 1=\delta_L$. Both induce $P_{\mathrm{obs}}$. Since $\hat\Delta$ is a function of the observed law, $\hat\Delta(P_1)=\hat\Delta(P_2)=:\hat\Delta_0$, and
\max_{k=1,2}(\hat\Delta_0-\Delta(P_k))^2\ge\left(\frac{\Delta(P_2)-\Delta(P_1)}{2}\right)^2=\frac{(1-p_H)^2}{4}.


The supremum dominates the maximum. Repeating with the roles of $L$ and $H$ interchanged yields the stated maximum; the bound is vacuous when both $p_g=1$. $\square$

Remark B.4.1 (Index convention). The full-completion, low-disclosure regime is placed in the high-pressure group. This is the only placement consistent with A2; placing it in the low-pressure group would violate monotonicity of disclosure in pressure and is inadmissible.

B.5 Theorem 3 (Sign reversal under informative censoring, race-dominant class)

Assume A3′ and $P\in\mathcal{P}(\mathcal{M}_{\mathrm{race}})$, so the type-conditional completion probabilities $\alpha_A(g),\alpha_B(g)$ are strictly decreasing in $g$. The observed conditional $g\mapsto P(Y=1\mid G=g)$ need not be decreasing.

Proof. Write $P(Y=1\mid G=g)=\mu(g)\alpha_A(g)+(1-\mu(g))\alpha_B(g)$ with $\mu(g)=P(u=A\mid G=g)$. Differentiation gives
\frac{d}{dg}P(Y=1\mid G=g)=\mu'(\alpha_A-\alpha_B)+\mu\alpha_A'+(1-\mu)\alpha_B'.


The last two terms are negative by A3′. The first is positive whenever $\mu'>0$ and $\alpha_A>\alpha_B$. Choose linear $\alpha_A=a_0-a_1g$, $\alpha_B=b_0-b_1g$ with $a_0>b_0$, $a_1,b_1>0$, $a_0-a_1g>b_0-b_1g$ on $[g_0,\bar{g}]$, and $\mu(g)=\mu_0+m(g-g_0)$ with $m>0$ large. Then the first term dominates near $g_0$ and the derivative is positive. Since $\mu$ is determined by the rival schedule and type-specific release policy, not by the causal effect, the observed sign is not informative about the structural sign. $\square$

Corollaries 1–2 follow: $\Delta$ does not identify the structural sign, and a gap to a release outside the frozen rival set does not adjudicate the hypothesis because the type-composition mechanism operates for any reference release.

