Appendix C. Proofs for Section 5

C.1 Theorem 4 (Sharp group bounds, including the zero-cell case)

Under A1, A2 and A5,
\Pi_g(\Lambda)=
\begin{cases}
\{0\}, & p_g=0,\ \Lambda<\infty,\\[2pt]
[0,1], & p_g=0,\ \Lambda=\infty,\\[2pt]
[p_g,\ \min\{1,p_g(1+\Lambda)\}], & p_g>0.
\end{cases}

Proof. For $p_g>0$, $a_g=\pi_g-p_g\ge 0$ and $a_g/p_g\le\Lambda$ give $\pi_g\le p_g(1+\Lambda)$ and $\pi_g\le 1$, while $\pi_g\ge p_g$. Sharpness: for any $\pi$ in the interval set $P(Y=1,M=1)=p_g$, $P(Y=1,M=0)=\pi-p_g$, $P(Y=0)=1-\pi$; all terms are non-negative, sum to one, and the implied odds are $(\pi-p_g)/p_g\le\Lambda$. For $p_g=0$ with $\Lambda<\infty$, any $a_g>0$ gives infinite odds, so $a_g=0$ and $\pi_g=0$; with $\Lambda=\infty$ there is no restriction, and any $\pi_g\in[0,1]$ is attainable by $M\equiv 0$. $\square$

C.2 Corollary 3 (Minkowski-difference bounds)

For $p_L,p_H>0$,
\Delta\in\big[\,p_L-\min\{1,p_H(1+\Lambda)\},\ \min\{1,p_L(1+\Lambda)\}-p_H\,\big].


The interval contains the identified set on any class satisfying A1, A2 and A5, and is sharp under A8.

Proof. The interval is the Minkowski difference of $\Pi_L$ and $\Pi_H$. Containment is immediate. Under A8 the group models factor, so every pair $(\pi_L,\pi_H)\in\Pi_L\times\Pi_H$ is jointly attainable and the Minkowski difference is the exact image under $(\pi_L,\pi_H)\mapsto\pi_L-\pi_H$. $\square$

C.3 Proposition 1 (Joint-law sharp bound)

With the observed joint law of $(Z_L,Z_H)$ fixed, the sharp identified set for $\Delta$ is the value set of the linear programme over the 16-cell table $\{P(M_L,Y_L,M_H,Y_H)\}$ subject to non-negativity, the observation constraints $P(M_g=1,Y_g=1)=p_g$, the odds constraints $P(M_g=0,Y_g=1)\le\Lambda P(M_g=1,Y_g=1)$, and any shared-dependence feasibility constraints. Because the objective is linear in the table, the extrema are attained at vertices of the polytope. The computation is $O(1)$ in the sample size.

C.4 Proposition 2 (Closed form for the breakdown bound)

For $p_L,p_H\in(0,1)$,
\Lambda^*=\frac{|p_L-p_H|}{\min\{p_L,p_H\}}.


If $p_g=0<p_{g'}$, then $\Lambda^*=\infty$ for every finite $\Lambda$.

Proof. $\underline{\Delta}(\Lambda)\le 0\iff p_H(1+\Lambda)\ge p_L\iff\Lambda\ge p_L/p_H-1$. $\overline{\Delta}(\Lambda)\ge 0\iff p_L(1+\Lambda)\ge p_H\iff\Lambda\ge p_H/p_L-1$. The larger threshold is $\max\{0,p_L/p_H-1,p_H/p_L-1\}=|p_L-p_H|/\min\{p_L,p_H\}$. If $p_H=0<p_L$, then $\underline{\Delta}=p_L>0$ for every finite $\Lambda$, so no finite $\Lambda$ produces an interval containing zero. $\square$

C.5 Corrected Proposition 3 (Monotone-selection refinement)

Assume additionally that the disclosure odds are ordered, $\lambda_H\le\lambda_L\le\Lambda$, and that the small-$\Lambda$ regime $p_g(1+\Lambda)\le 1$ holds for $g=L,H$. Then the sharp identified set for $\Delta$ under the ordering is
\Delta\in\big[\,(p_L-p_H)(1+\Lambda),\ p_L(1+\Lambda)-p_H\,\big]
\quad\text{when }p_L\le p_H,


\Delta\in\big[\,p_L-p_H,\ p_L(1+\Lambda)-p_H\,\big]
\quad\text{when }p_L>p_H.

Proof. The ordering constraint is $\lambda_H\le\lambda_L\iff\pi_L/p_L\ge\pi_H/p_H$. The feasible polytope in $(\pi_L,\pi_H)$ has vertices $(p_L,p_H)$, $(p_L(1+\Lambda),p_H)$, $(p_L(1+\Lambda),p_H(1+\Lambda))$; the point $(p_L,p_H(1+\Lambda))$ violates the ordering. When $p_L\le p_H$, the minimum of $\Delta$ is at the common upper vertex $(p_L(1+\Lambda),p_H(1+\Lambda))$, giving $(p_L-p_H)(1+\Lambda)$; when $p_L>p_H$, the minimum is at $(p_L,p_H)$, giving $p_L-p_H$. The maximum is always at $(p_L(1+\Lambda),p_H)$, giving $p_L(1+\Lambda)-p_H$. Non-emptiness holds because $(p_L-p_H)(1+\Lambda)\le p_L(1+\Lambda)-p_H\iff -p_H\Lambda\le 0$. $\square$

Lemma C.5.1 (Large-$\Lambda$ regime). If $p_L(1+\Lambda)>1$ and $p_H(1+\Lambda)>1$, vertex enumeration with the probability cap at $1$ gives $[p_L/p_H-1,\,1-p_H]$ when $p_L\le p_H$, and $[p_L-p_H,\,1-p_H/p_L]$ when $p_L>p_H$. The small-$\Lambda$ regime is the primary one for the design.

C.6 Numerical illustration

With $p_L=0.50$ and $p_H=0.167$ at $\Lambda=1$: $\Pi_L=[0.50,1.00]$, $\Pi_H=[0.167,0.333]$; unconstrained $\Delta\in[0.167,0.833]$; ordered (since $p_L>p_H$) $\Delta\in[0.333,0.833]$; $\Lambda^*=0.333/0.167=2.00$.

C.7 Rival-set sensitivity

Definition 4 defines $G_i^{\mathrm{frozen}}$ and $G_i^{\mathrm{dyn}}(h)$. Definition 5 defines the fragility index
\Phi(h)=\mathbf{1}\{\text{interval sign differs}\}\vee\mathbf{1}\{\Lambda^{*,\mathrm{frozen}}\neq\Lambda^{*,\mathrm{dyn}}\}.


$\Phi(h)=1$ means the inferential content depends on the rival-set definition, and the finding is reported as fragile to rival-set definition. The decomposition is descriptive and receives no $p$-value. Algorithm 7 computes both exposures, runs Algorithm 2 under each, and evaluates $\Phi(h)$ in $O(|\mathcal{R}|^2)$ time.

