Appendix E. Proofs for Section 7

E.1 Theorem 6 (Exactness on the observed outcomes, with orbit feasibility)

Under $H_0^Z$ and $p_{\mathrm{perm}}=|\Omega(W)|^{-1}\sum_{w\in\Omega(W)}\mathbf{1}\{|T(w,Z)|\ge|T(W,Z)|\}$, for any $\alpha\in(0,1)$,
P_{H_0^Z}(p_{\mathrm{perm}}\le\alpha)\le\alpha.


The test is non-trivial at level $\alpha$ only if $|\Omega(W)|\ge 1/\alpha$; if $|\Omega(W)|<1/\alpha$, then $p_{\mathrm{perm}}>\alpha$ almost surely.

Proof. Under $H_0^Z$, $Z$ is fixed and $W$ is uniform on $\Omega$. The rank of $|T(W,Z)|$ among $\{|T(w,Z)|:w\in\Omega\}$ is uniform on $\{1,\dots,|\Omega|\}$, so $p_{\mathrm{perm}}$ equals rank$/|\Omega|$ and $P(p_{\mathrm{perm}}\le\alpha)=\lfloor\alpha|\Omega|\rfloor/|\Omega|\le\alpha$. The smallest attainable $p_{\mathrm{perm}}$ is $1/|\Omega|$, so if $|\Omega|<1/\alpha$ no rejection is possible. $\square$

E.2 Corollary 6 (No power against the structural hypothesis)

The permutation test of Definition 9 has power equal to its size against any alternative in which $Y_i$ differs from $Z_i$ but $Z_i$ is unchanged.

Proof. $T$ is a function of $Z$ alone, so two data-generating processes with the same $Z$-law produce the same statistic and the same $p$-value; power equals size. $\square$

E.3 Proposition 4 (Necessity of studentization)

Under $H_0^Z$ with $\sigma_1^2\neq\sigma_0^2$ and unequal group sizes, the unstudentized statistic $T_0=\hat\Delta$ has asymptotic size strictly greater than $\alpha$ for $\alpha<1/2$ along $n_k\to\infty$, $n_{1k}/n_k\to c_k\in(0,1)$.

Proof sketch. The permutation variance of $\hat\Delta$ uses the pooled variance, $\sigma^2(1/c+1/(1-c))/n$, while the sampling variance is $\sigma_1^2/(nc)+\sigma_0^2/(n(1-c))$. These coincide only when $\sigma_1^2=\sigma_0^2$; otherwise the permutation distribution is too narrow or too wide and the test over- or under-rejects. Studentization replaces the pooled variance with group-specific estimates and restores asymptotic pivoting. $\square$

E.4 Proposition 5 (Bootstrap failure with few treated clusters)

The cluster-robust variance estimator is downward-biased by a factor of order $K_1/(K_1-1)$, and the Rademacher wild cluster bootstrap has support of size $2^{K_1}$, so it cannot resolve tail mass below $1/2^{K_1}$; when $2^{K_1}$ is small, size is bounded away from $\alpha$ uniformly in a neighbourhood of the null.

Proof sketch. The bias follows from the leverage of the cluster mean on the squared cluster residual. The bootstrap failure follows from the support size of the Rademacher weights: if $2^{K_1}$ is small enough that the $(1-\alpha)$ quantile cannot resolve the tail at $\alpha$, size control fails. The numerical threshold is a conventional heuristic, not a theoretical constant. $\square$

Design consequence. Randomization inference is primary, studentization is retained, the wild cluster bootstrap is reported as a descriptive complement, and the orbit-size condition of Theorem 6 governs whether the randomization test is non-trivial at all.

