Appendix F. Minimum Detectable Effect

F.1 Theorem 7 (Fisher approximation)

With $z=\mathrm{arctanh}(\hat\rho)\sim\mathcal{N}(\mathrm{arctanh}\rho,1/(n-3))$ approximately,
\rho_{\min}(n)=\tanh\left(\frac{z_{1-\alpha/2}+z_{1-\beta}}{\sqrt{n-3}}\right).

Proof. The two-sided level-$\alpha$ test rejects when $|z|\ge z_{1-\alpha/2}$, and power $1-\beta$ is attained when $z_\rho=\mathrm{arctanh}(\rho)\sqrt{n-3}=z_{1-\alpha/2}+z_{1-\beta}$. Inverting the transform gives the stated expression. $\square$

At $\alpha=0.05$ and $1-\beta=0.80$, $z_{1-\alpha/2}+z_{1-\beta}=2.802$. Corrected values: $n=10$, $\rho_{\min}=0.785$; $n=12$, $0.732$; $n=15$, $0.670$; $n=18$, $0.620$; $n=20$, $0.591$.

F.2 Remark 4 (Finite-sample refinement)

The exact finite-sample MDE is $\rho_{\min}^{\mathrm{exact}}(n)=\inf\{|\rho|:\pi_n(\rho,\alpha)\ge 1-\beta\}$, where $\pi_n$ is the exact permutation power computed from the hypergeometric distribution of permutation ranks under the null and the non-central distribution under the alternative. For $n=12$–$20$ and $\alpha=0.05$, the exact MDE exceeds the Fisher value by approximately $5$–$15$ per cent depending on tie structure and stratification; hence $n=12$ gives roughly $0.79$–$0.82$ and $n=20$ roughly $0.64$–$0.68$. The Fisher values are lower bounds on the MDE, not estimates.

F.3 Corollary 7 (Interpretation)

An observed $|\hat\rho|$ below $\rho_{\min}^{\mathrm{exact}}(n)$ is reported as inconclusive, not as null; a confidence interval containing $\rho_{\min}^{\mathrm{exact}}(n)$ does not exclude a substantively important effect. The design is low-powered by construction, and this low power is itself a statement about the record: at the admissible sample sizes the record can only detect near-deterministic associations.

